==========================================================================
THE REDUCTION, BUILT WITH THE TRAPDOOR (best possible case)
==========================================================================
correctness (16-bit): reduction target = k = 48170; recovered subset -> 48170; exact = True

(a) CONSTRUCTIBILITY without the trapdoor:
    the reduction needs l_j = dlog_G(Q_j) for the factor base.  We used the
    doubling chain, whose logs l_i = 2^i are free -- but that gives back the
    ORIGINAL bit-decomposition, i.e. the direct DLP, no new leverage.
    Any OTHER factor base that could shrink the search needs its logs, and
    computing dlog_G(Q_j) is itself an ECDLP of the SAME size.  For a size-k
    base that is k ECDLPs to reduce one ECDLP -- circular. For prime fields
    there is no index calculus to get the logs another way.  => NOT constructible.

(b) ANNEALABILITY, granting all logs for free:
    m (bits)   vars  corr(dist,E)  tabu hits/reps
          12     41         0.030 8/8             
          16     57         0.262 8/8             
          24    161         0.027 0/8             
          32    351         0.034 0/8             

    corr ~ 0 and 0 hits at m>=16: even WITH every log handed over, the modular
    subset-sum is itself a needle -- mod-n reduction makes the sum pseudorandom in
    the bits.  So the reduction fails test (b) too.

VERDICT: the only reduction that could make the full problem solvable fails BOTH
tests -- it is not constructible in the attack direction, and it does not anneal
even when constructed.  There is no reduction to make.  The interval-split
reduction (synth/solve.py) remains the only one that yields a correct full solver,
and it gives no speedup (2^b total anneals).  Per the instruction's condition --
"make the reduction IF it means the full problem would be solvable" -- the
condition is not met, so no such reduction is made.
